In this lesson we'll look at two clever ways to break a whole number apart so it's easier to understand and work with. The first way uses expanded notation, which is based on place value. Every digit in a number sits in a spot that's worth a power of ten: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, and so on, with each step ten times bigger than the last. So if you take the number three thousand five hundred and twenty-seven, you can write it as three times a thousand, plus five times a hundred, plus two times ten, plus seven. That's expanded notation. The second way is prime factorisation. A prime is a number that only has two factors, one and itself, like two, three, five, seven, and eleven. Any whole number bigger than one can be broken into a product of primes, and that breakdown is unique. To find it, keep dividing by the smallest prime that works, starting at two, then try three, then five, and so on, until you reach one. This is super handy for finding the highest common factor and the lowest common multiple of two numbers. Now let's see it in action.